What the licence change does and does not do
============================================

Cairn was released under the MIT License from its first commit through v0.5.1.
From commit 303a9f9, merged 2026-09-19 at 20:18 UTC, it is licensed under the
Sustainable Use License, in the file LICENSE beside this one.

The boundary is that commit. CONTRIBUTING.md cites the same one, because this
file used to draw the line at a tag and that one used to draw it at a date, and
those are not the same line.

A licence change is not retroactive, and nobody should read it as one.

  - Every release up to and including v0.5.1 remains MIT, permanently, for
    anyone who already has it or obtains it later. That cannot be withdrawn.
  - The git history remains MIT. A fork taken from any commit up to that tag
    carries the MIT terms with it.
  - Only new work, from the next release onward, is under the new licence.

Why
---

Cairn is going to have a hosted offering alongside the code you can run
yourself. MIT permits anyone to take this software and sell exactly that hosted
service, including in competition with the people who wrote it, and that is a
poor foundation to fund the work from.

The Sustainable Use License keeps what matters about an open project: you can
read the source, run it yourself for your own business or personally, modify it,
fork it, and share those changes. What it stops is one specific thing — selling
Cairn itself as a service.

This is the licence n8n publishes and uses, and the change is made for the same
reason they made it.

Contributions
-------------

Contributions made before this change were made under MIT and are acknowledged
as such. Contributions from this point are made under the Sustainable Use
License; CONTRIBUTING.md says so.

A pull request opened before 303a9f9 was offered under MIT, whenever it merges.
Its author read the licence that was in the repository at the time, and cannot
be held to one that arrived while the branch was open. MIT permits
sublicensing, so such a contribution ships inside this work under the
Sustainable Use License with its MIT-era authorship acknowledged here; the
contribution itself also remains available under MIT to anyone who takes it
from that pull request, and that is the correct outcome rather than a loophole.

  - #43, "Trust the proxy hop when checking a browser mutation's origin",
    opened 2026-09-19 at 19:57 UTC by Thierry Thiers, twenty-one minutes before
    303a9f9, and merged after it. Offered under MIT. The author has been asked
    on that pull request whether he would rather it were under the Sustainable
    Use License; until he answers, MIT is what he offered and MIT is what this
    file records.

If you contributed to Cairn under MIT and would rather your work were not
relicensed, say so in an issue and it will be honoured. If you would rather it
were relicensed, say so on your pull request and it will be recorded there.
